johannac:{n:"Johanna Cornelia de Vries",d:"c. 1788–1860",b:1788,x:1860,s:"b",p:["gerardusdv", "idaj"],r:["nl"],t:"The daughter of Gerardus de Vries and Ida Jonkers. She married Bernardus van den Berg of Tilburg and died there on 22 January 1860, aged 72.",src:[["Death record, 1860", "https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ab7d4fe8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
gerardus:{n:"Gerardus Hamers",d:"c. 1799–1849",b:1799,x:1849,s:"b",p:["johanneshamers", "geertruij"],r:["nl"],t:"Of Tilburg, the son of Johannes Hamers and Geertruij Reijnen. On 25 November 1818, aged 19, he married Maria van Gestel, who was 33. Their daughter Maria Gertruda was born in 1829. He died at Tilburg on 6 December 1849, aged 50.",src:[["Marriage, 1818", "https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1d15bcae-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"], ["Death record, 1849", "https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a6497d94-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
mariavg:{n:"Maria van Gestel",d:"b. c. 1785",b:1785,s:"b",p:["petervg", "johannacdl"],r:["nl"],t:"Of Tilburg, the daughter of Peter van Gestel and Johanna Catharina de Lepper. She married Gerardus Hamers in 1818, fourteen years her junior.",src:[["Marriage, 1818", "https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1d15bcae-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
herbert:{"n":"Herbert Anthony","d":"c. 1836–1897 · Sebastopol","b":1836,"x":1897,"s":"f","p":["davidanthony","annejones"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"The son of David Anthony and Anne Jones, a family with Welsh names. He married Mara James in Victoria in 1867, and their daughter Matilda was born at Sebastopol, on the Ballarat goldfield, in 1873. The family later lived at Stawell, another gold town, where Herbert was a miner. In 1887 the Stawell Prospecting Board, which paid out government money to search for new gold, granted £100 to \"Herbert Anthony and party\" to prospect an old lead at Clifton Rock. His elder brother Thomas, a coal miner who lived alone at Hamilton near Newcastle, NSW, died in 1879 and left his property to Herbert.\n\nMara died at Stawell in 1893 and Herbert in 1897, aged 61. His will left everything to his two children, Albert and Matilda, but said the estate was not to be divided for eight years, and that if no tombstone had been put over his grave by then, his executors must spend between £10 and £30 on one. In 1901 a Melbourne judge ruled that once the tombstone was up, Albert and Matilda could have their shares straight away.","src":[["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Stawell Prospecting Board, Age, 9 Feb 1887","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article197459719"],["Will case, Age, 20 Jul 1901","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article190029801"],["Thomas Anthony, Sydney Morning Herald, 26 Feb 1879","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article13429904"]]},
mara:{n:"Mara James",d:"c. 1840–1893",b:1840,x:1893,s:"f",p:["jamesevan", "maryevans"],r:["uk", "au"],t:"The daughter of James Evan James and Mary Evans. She married Herbert Anthony in Victoria in 1867 and was the mother of Matilda (1873). She died at Stawell in 1893, aged 53.",src:[["Victorian BDM history search", "https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
josephwil:{"n":"Joseph Wilson","d":"c. 1839–1887","b":1839,"x":1887,"s":"p","p":["johnwil","marthab"],"r":["au"],"t":"He married Sarah Whatmough, daughter of the Plenty River orchardist Robert Whatmough, in Victoria in 1866, and they raised their family at Diamond Creek, a small farming and gold-mining district north-east of Melbourne, where their children included John, the eldest son, Martha (born 1869) and James Emmeth. He died at Diamond Creek in 1887, aged 48, leaving Sarah with the family. In May 1893 his eldest son John, \"eldest son of the late Joseph Wilson, Diamond Creek\", married Sarah Jane Harrington of the same place at St Katherine's, St Helena.","src":[["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Wilson–Harrington marriage, Age, 6 Jun 1893","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/193421412"]]},
sarahw:{"n":"Sarah Whatmough","d":"c. 1846–1934","b":1846,"x":1934,"s":"p","p":["robertwh","maryhill"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"The daughter of Robert Whatmough, the Rochdale-born fruit grower of the Plenty River whose apples won prizes in Melbourne in the 1850s and whose peach, Whatmough's Seedling, was grown across Victoria. She grew up among his orchards near Greensborough and Diamond Creek, and married Joseph Wilson in 1866. They raised their family at Diamond Creek, where their children included John, the eldest son, Martha (Grandma Parky, born 1869) and James Emmeth. Joseph died in 1887, leaving her with the family; in one bad snake season the local paper reported a snake killed at Mrs Joseph Wilson's at Diamond Creek. She outlived her husband by 47 years and died at Diamond Creek in 1934, aged 88.","src":[["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Snakes at Diamond Creek, Evelyn Observer","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/64027537"]]},
johnwil:{n:"John Wilson",d:"",s:"p",p:[],r:["uk"]},
